      "id": 1,
      "text": "Norway, Michigan got its name from a forest of Norway pines that surrounded the original town site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s roots lie in the late‑1800s iron ore boom that forged Norway, Kingsford, and Iron Mountain into a mining hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ironmountain.org/pages/history-of-the-area/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s Henry Ford built a factory in nearby Kingsford that produced wooden Model T cars, charcoal briquettes, and WWII gliders.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ironmountain.org/pages/history-of-the-area/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The World War II Glider and Military Museum in Iron Mountain houses one of only seven fully restored CG‑4A gliders in the world.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ironmountain.org/pages/history-of-the-area/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Jake Menghini Historical Museum in Norway showcases the town’s mining past and local heritage through artifacts and exhibits.",
